<div role="alert" class="mb-5 rounded-2xl border border-amber-300 bg-amber-50 p-4 text-sm leading-6 text-amber-950">
    <strong>Draft — not an approved institutional policy.</strong>
    PIT’s Data Protection Officer contact, approved retention schedule, and final privacy procedures must be confirmed before this information is treated as official.
</div>

<article class="space-y-6 rounded-3xl bg-white p-5 text-sm leading-7 text-slate-700 shadow-sm ring-1 ring-slate-200 sm:p-8">
    <section>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900">Information this system handles</h3>
        <p class="mt-2">The system may process your name, email address, contact number, requester category (student, faculty, staff or outsider), office or organization, reservation schedules, requested venues and equipment, participant counts, supporting documents, identification submitted for account verification, payment receipt references for outsiders, approval actions, notifications and related activity history. The fields requested vary by account type and form.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900">Why the information is used</h3>
        <p class="mt-2">Information is used to create and verify accounts, check venue and equipment availability, evaluate and approve requests, coordinate equipment, record payments, send status updates and reminders, keep a request history, and protect the service from misuse. The institution must confirm any additional purposes before publication.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900">Identification for account verification</h3>
        <p class="mt-2">If you register as a student, faculty member or staff member without a PIT email address, or as an outsider, you may be asked to upload an ID (a PIT ID, or a government-issued ID such as a National ID, driver’s license or passport). You may cover your ID number; only your name and photo are needed.</p>
        <p class="mt-2">The ID image is stored privately and can be viewed only by authorized Supply Office staff, and every view is logged. <strong>The image is deleted as soon as it is approved or rejected.</strong> The system keeps only the type of ID, the result of the review, who reviewed it and when.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900">Who may access it</h3>
        <p class="mt-2">Access is limited by account role and assigned responsibility. Requestors can view their own requests; assigned custodians can review requests for their venues or equipment; the IGP office sees payment details for outsider requests; and the Supply Office performs review, verification and final approval. Public calendar information is limited and does not show private requestor contact details.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900">Documents and approval records</h3>
        <p class="mt-2">Supporting documents may contain personal information and are used only for request review. Approvals are recorded under each approver’s account; the printed form lists the role, action, date and an approval reference, not personal names or signature images. The QR code on a printed form opens a verification page that shows only the control number, schedule, venue, status and approval references. Do not upload information that is not needed for your request.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900">Storage and retention</h3>
        <p class="mt-2">ID images are deleted after review, as described above. For other records, the hosting location, backup schedule, retention period and secure-disposal procedures must be confirmed by PIT.</p>
        <p class="mt-2 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-3"><strong>To be completed by PIT:</strong> Identify the approved records-retention schedule, storage location, backup controls, and deletion or archival process.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900">Your rights and privacy requests</h3>
        <p class="mt-2">Under the Data Privacy Act of 2012 (Republic Act No. 10173), you have the right to be informed, to access, to object, to correct, to have data erased or blocked, to data portability, to file a complaint, and to damages, subject to the law’s conditions and exceptions.</p>
        <p class="mt-2 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-3"><strong>To be completed by PIT:</strong> Name of the Data Protection Officer, office address, email address and the process for privacy requests and complaints.</p>
    </section>
    <p class="border-t border-slate-200 pt-4 text-xs text-slate-500">This draft is informational and is not legal advice. The institution must review and approve the final notice, lawful bases, contact details, recipients, retention, and complaint process.</p>
</article>
